# The question

For a brand-new environment with no existing database, tooling, or operational commitments, where should PostgreSQL live? Neither option is a thesis to defend here — each criterion below collects what both sides have to say, and the context section names the conditions that swing the verdict. Node color marks which side an argument serves; the arrows carry the valence.

[CNPG]: Run PostgreSQL in-cluster with the CloudNativePG operator — the database becomes a declarative Kubernetes workload living in the same git repository, pipeline, and operational plane as everything it serves. #cnpg
  -> [RDS]

[RDS]: Provision a managed database service such as AWS RDS — the provider carries storage, failover execution, patching, and the pager behind a bounded service interface. #rds
  -> [CNPG]


# Cost

<No managed-service premium>: Managed Postgres bills a substantial markup over the underlying compute and storage, around the clock, per instance; CNPG pods run on cluster capacity you already operate, and the premium compounds across every environment, service, and replica as the fleet grows. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]
  - <Engineering time is the real cost>: Total cost of ownership includes the humans — building, testing, and carrying the pager for self-managed Postgres consumes engineer time worth far more than the instance markup, and a production database usually needs dedicated, tainted node pools anyway, eroding the "free capacity" claim. #rds
    - <Expertise cost is symmetric>: Managed is not expertise-free — an RDS estate demands fluency in AWS primitives and Terraform (VPCs, subnet and parameter groups, IAM auth wiring, secrets rotation, alarm tuning) just as CNPG demands Kubernetes and its CRDs; these are comparable declarative toolchains, so expertise differentiates neither option and the real question is which one the team already lives in. #cnpg
      -> <Spend innovation tokens on product>
      - <Pager asymmetry survives>: The two toolchains are equivalent for declaring a database but not for operating one — the managed premium buys the provider's on-call for storage failures, failover execution, and patching of the running system behind a bounded service interface, so even with declaration fluency a wash, the rare-but-deep operational tail (Postgres internals plus CSI plus scheduler, simultaneously, at 3 a.m.) still lands only on whoever self-hosts. #rds
        - <On-call is purchasable a la carte>: Third parties sell exactly this — CYBERTEC offers 24/7, SLA-backed CloudNativePG support covering EKS and other managed Kubernetes, and EDB (CNPG's originator) sells commercial support — so "someone else's on-call" can be unbundled from the hosting substrate and bought at whatever tier the team needs. #cnpg
          - <A contract advises, a service operates>: Managed remediation is built in and silent — failed hardware is replaced and failover executed without anyone opening a ticket — while a support contract still requires your team to detect, engage, and act, and its fee narrows the very cost gap that motivates self-hosting. #rds

# Day-2 operations

<You own day-2 operations>: Backup verification, restore drills, failover testing, vacuum and bloat management, version upgrades, and the 3 a.m. page all belong to the team, permanently — a heavy standing tax on precisely the kind of small team a greenfield project usually has. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <CNPG automates the mechanics>: CNPG is a mature CNCF operator with automated failover, switchover, scheduled backups to object storage, point-in-time recovery, and rolling minor upgrades built in; the residual burden is owning and rehearsing these capabilities, not building them. #cnpg
    + <Restore drills become pipelines>: Because recovery is declarative — a fresh Cluster bootstrapped from the object-storage backup of a production one — restore verification can run as a scheduled pipeline that stands up yesterday's database and checks it, turning the discipline most teams skip into ordinary CI. #cnpg

<Stateful-on-Kubernetes sharp edges>: Storage classes, zone-locked volumes, pod eviction, disruption budgets, node-pool upgrades, and CSI driver bugs interact with database durability in ways that produce rare, high-severity failure modes at the intersection of two complex systems — and every postmortem lands on you. #rds
  +> [RDS]

<The operator is a dependency too>: CNPG itself is software on the critical path — operator upgrades, CRD schema migrations, bugs, and CVEs arrive on the project's schedule and must be tracked, tested, and rolled out; the managed service has no analogous customer-visible layer to maintain. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <Data plane outlives control plane>: An operator outage degrades automation, not service — running clusters keep accepting connections and replicating while the operator is down or upgrading, so the added dependency threatens convenience features, not availability. #cnpg

<Performance isolation is on you>: On shared nodes the database competes with every workload for CPU, memory, and disk bandwidth — kubelet memory pressure, OOM kills, CPU throttling, and noisy-neighbour IO become database incidents; a managed instance's resources are its own by construction. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <QoS is table stakes>: Guaranteed QoS classes, pod anti-affinity, and a dedicated tainted node pool are a handful of lines in the same manifests and eliminate those contention classes wholesale — a solved pattern, though one that concedes the database is not running on spare capacity. #cnpg

# Team and focus

<Spend innovation tokens on product>: A new venture has a limited budget of novel operational risk, and the boring-technology argument says spend it on the product, not the substrate — a managed database is a credit card and a checkbox, leaving the team's full attention on shipping. #rds
  +> [RDS]

<Kubernetes is a prerequisite>: CNPG presupposes a production-grade Kubernetes cluster with competent operators; a greenfield that would not otherwise run Kubernetes is adopting the industry's most complex runtime as an entry fee for its least forgiving workload. #rds
  +> [RDS]

<Rarer expertise and bus factor>: Operating Postgres well on Kubernetes demands a scarcer skill set than consuming RDS, concentrates that knowledge in whoever built it, and makes hiring and handover harder for the system least tolerant of improvisation. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <AI compresses the expertise gap>: In the age of Claude-class AI assistants, deep Postgres-on-Kubernetes knowledge is available on demand — for architecture review, runbook authoring, and mid-incident diagnosis — so the scarcity premium on specialist expertise, and the bus factor of the one engineer who built the system, shrink every year. #cnpg
    - <AI staffs no rota>: AI assistance is symmetric — it derisks operating a managed estate just as much — and in an incident someone accountable must still be awake to validate and execute its advice against production data; it thins the knowledge barrier but provides neither an on-call rota nor rehearsed muscle memory. #rds

# Resilience and blast radius

<Shared blast radius>: An in-cluster database lives inside the cluster's failure domain, so a botched cluster upgrade, a control-plane outage, or an over-eager GitOps prune can take the data layer down with every application; a managed instance survives the loss of the entire cluster. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <Guardrails, not substrate>: An unprotected managed instance is one bad terraform destroy away from the same data loss — deletion protection, final snapshots, and backup retention are opt-in configuration on RDS exactly as backup schedules, volume reclaim policies, and prune protection are on Kubernetes, so surviving an operator mistake depends on configuring the guardrails, not on which substrate holds the data. #cnpg
    - <Fate-sharing is not a config error>: Guardrails equalise only the deletion scenario — even a perfectly configured in-cluster database still shares fate with cluster upgrades, control-plane outages, and node-pool churn, a coupling that exists regardless of configuration and has no analogue on a managed instance, which survives the loss of the entire cluster. #rds

# The managed bundle

<Managed ecosystem lost>: IAM-integrated authentication, automatic credential rotation, query performance insights, patching inside maintenance windows, one-click read replicas, and cross-region disaster recovery all come with the managed service and must each be rebuilt, replaced, or consciously dropped. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <The cluster ecosystem refills most of it>: CNPG ships a Prometheus exporter, declarative read replicas, and cross-region replica clusters fed from object storage, while operator-managed TLS and external-secrets rotation cover auth hygiene — most "lost" items are integration work with cluster-native equivalents, not absences. #cnpg

<Compliance is easier on managed>: Auditors and compliance platforms have ready-made, API-attested evidence for managed databases (encryption at rest, backup retention, audit logging); a self-hosted database means designing, implementing, and evidencing those controls yourself before certification. #rds
  +> [RDS]
  - <GitOps is an audit trail>: Desired state, change history, approvals, and rollbacks are already evidenced in git, and policy-as-code admission control plus CIS benchmarks yield machine-checkable attestations — the evidence is differently shaped rather than absent, though the team, not the provider, is the attesting party. #cnpg

# Portability, workflow, and control

<Portability and no lock-in>: A CNPG cluster runs identically on any conformant Kubernetes — any cloud, on-prem, or a laptop — so the platform's most gravity-laden component stays portable instead of anchoring you to one provider's regions, pricing, and roadmap. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]
  - <Portability is speculative>: Most organisations never exercise the multi-cloud option; paying an operational premium every day for an exit right rarely used is a poor trade unless provider exit is a concrete requirement. #rds
    - <Not always speculative>: For on-prem, edge, air-gapped, or sovereign-cloud deployments there is no managed Postgres to buy — if the roadmap includes any of them, portability is exercised on day one and the exit right is a requirement, not insurance. #cnpg

<GitOps-native from day one>: Database topology, users, backup schedules, and restore sources are declarative custom resources living in the same repository and pipeline as the workloads that consume them, so an entire environment — data layer included — is reproducible from git. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]

<Dev-prod parity>: Every environment from a laptop to production runs the same Postgres images with the same extensions and configuration, eliminating the class of bugs and tuning surprises that come from developing against a database that only exists in the cloud. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]

<Full Postgres control>: Superuser access, any extension, any minor or major version the day it ships, and tuning applied as declarative config with rolling restarts — no extension allowlists, forced maintenance windows, version lag, or parameter-group indirection. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]
  - <Control is a footgun>: The constraints being escaped double as guardrails — superuser access, unvetted extensions, and day-one major versions are classic sources of self-inflicted outages, and a vendor that refuses risky actions is a feature for a team without a DBA. #rds

<One operational plane>: One observability and security stack serves everything — the database emits Prometheus metrics, obeys network policies and RBAC, and draws secrets from the same tooling as the apps, instead of adding a second console, IAM model, and alerting pipeline that must be integrated and kept coherent. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]

<Cheap ephemeral databases>: A Cluster CR stamps out per-branch, per-test, or per-tenant databases in seconds and destroys them just as fast, where managed instances take tens of minutes to create and bill by the hour — transforming preview environments, CI, and load testing. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]
  - <Ephemeral wins do not need prod CNPG>: Preview, CI, and load-test databases can run on CNPG while the system of record stays managed — the benefit argues for CNPG somewhere in the stack, not for the primary, so it weighs lightly on this decision. #rds
    +> [Start managed, revisit at scale]
    - <Clones follow the substrate>: The split holds only while previews run on seed data — cloning is smooth same-substrate (CNPG-to-CNPG via backups, base-backup streaming, or copy-on-write volume snapshots; RDS-to-RDS via snapshots), so the day ephemeral environments need production-like data, a managed primary feeding in-cluster clones pays the clunkiest path of all (dump and restore, snapshot export, or cross-substrate logical replication), and the ephemeral fleet starts pulling the primary toward its own substrate. #cnpg

# Timing and data gravity

<Greenfield is the cheapest moment>: There is no migration, no cutover window, no legacy auth or monitoring to rebuild — auth, backups, observability, and runbooks are designed once, CNPG-shaped, from day one; choosing managed now and switching later means paying the full data-gravity cost of a live migration. #cnpg
  +> [CNPG]

# Cross-cutting context

The decision is mostly a question about the team, not the technology — and the platform-maturity consideration cuts in opposite directions depending on the team, so it is stated as two mutually exclusive conditions.

[Kubernetes is already home]: Kubernetes is already the platform's centre of gravity and real SRE capacity is on staff — the substrate adds no novel operational risk, and the strongest objections lose most of their force. #context
  +> <Expertise cost is symmetric>
  -> <Kubernetes is a prerequisite>
  -> <You own day-2 operations>

[Kubernetes would be new]: The team has no production Kubernetes experience — the cluster itself is an innovation token spent before the database question even arises, and the strongest objections are close to decisive. #context
  +> <Kubernetes is a prerequisite>
  +> <You own day-2 operations>
  -> <Expertise cost is symmetric>

[Data gravity makes it semi-permanent]: Whichever substrate the data lands on, moving it later is the hardest migration in the stack — so this choice deserves deliberate analysis now, and "we can always change it later" should carry little weight in either direction. #context
  +> <Greenfield is the cheapest moment>
  +> <Deferral is not free>


# A middle path

[Start managed, revisit at scale]: Begin on a managed database to spend early innovation tokens on the product, use CNPG freely for ephemeral and non-critical databases to build operational confidence, and revisit the primary only when scale makes the managed premium material. #alternative
  -> [CNPG]
  -> [RDS]
  - <Deferral is not free>: Because of data gravity, "revisit at scale" usually means "never, or as an expensive migration project" — by the time the premium is material, the database is at its largest, busiest, and most entangled, which is the worst moment to move it. #cnpg
